Dear 3DPrint.com Editors,

Solidon3D 0.2.2 targets a common desktop-printing problem that sits between
mesh repair and full CAD: the downloaded model is valid, but one functional
detail is wrong.

The most direct example is a mounting hole. Solidon recognises the existing
cylindrical feature in an imported STL or 3MF, preserves its location and
axis, and changes the diameter as a reversible operation. A standards-based
heat-set insert, screw seat or bearing seat can then be added using clearance
values measured from a small printed calibration piece rather than copied from
a generic table.

Version 0.2.2 also adds matching printable screws and nuts, more capable
in-viewport sketching and per-spool process data that reaches the correct 3MF
material slot and external slicer. Reusable parametric components can be made
from history steps without embedding executable code in the project.

The application runs locally on Windows, macOS and Linux, with no account,
telemetry or cloud project store. An optional language model may call the
fixed operation API, but all geometry is calculated by deterministic code.

Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Detailed changes: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

For editorial evaluation I can provide the original STL, measured
before-and-after files, the released 0.2.2 build and high-resolution images.

